1
00:00:04,307 --> 00:00:05,227
[SPEAKER_02]: Let's make sure they're right.

2
00:00:07,288 --> 00:00:09,409
[SPEAKER_02]: August 28, 1898 in St. Louis?

3
00:00:09,549 --> 00:00:10,329
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

4
00:00:10,349 --> 00:00:11,269
[SPEAKER_02]: St. Louis, Missouri.

5
00:00:11,289 --> 00:00:12,870
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you come from a ball-playing family?

6
00:00:13,130 --> 00:00:13,370
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

7
00:00:13,870 --> 00:00:14,691
[SPEAKER_02]: Dad didn't play ball?

8
00:00:14,891 --> 00:00:16,031
[SPEAKER_02]: A bunch of house painters.

9
00:00:16,271 --> 00:00:17,772
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that right?

10
00:00:18,212 --> 00:00:18,932
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

11
00:00:19,152 --> 00:00:20,713
[SPEAKER_02]: Where'd your talent first emerge?

12
00:00:20,733 --> 00:00:21,513
[SPEAKER_02]: Huh?

13
00:00:21,613 --> 00:00:22,954
[SPEAKER_02]: Where'd your talent first emerge?

14
00:00:22,974 --> 00:00:24,674
[SPEAKER_02]: You play in high school or Sandlot?

15
00:00:24,694 --> 00:00:25,995
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I never went to high school.

16
00:00:28,676 --> 00:00:30,816
[SPEAKER_01]: My father wanted me to be a house painter too, you know.

17
00:00:33,555 --> 00:00:36,257
[SPEAKER_01]: But I wanted to be a ball player.

18
00:00:36,317 --> 00:00:40,539
[SPEAKER_01]: And I only went to about the fifth grade public school.

19
00:00:41,719 --> 00:00:47,402
[SPEAKER_01]: And I always did contend that I could have went to the sixth grade, but I didn't want to pass the old man.

20
00:00:47,562 --> 00:00:48,202
[SPEAKER_01]: Embarrassed.

21
00:00:49,583 --> 00:00:52,444
[SPEAKER_01]: But I was satisfied.

22
00:00:52,564 --> 00:00:53,325
[SPEAKER_01]: I went to work.

23
00:00:53,365 --> 00:00:54,485
[SPEAKER_01]: I had many jobs.

24
00:00:55,246 --> 00:00:56,746
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was with Connie Mack in 1960.

25
00:00:58,768 --> 00:01:00,428
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, did you play ball in St. Louis?

26
00:01:00,468 --> 00:01:05,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, St. Louis Screw Company, the old St. Louis Screw Company in the Municipal League.

27
00:01:06,349 --> 00:01:10,770
[SPEAKER_01]: And I played for the Campbell Auto Supply Company.

28
00:01:11,050 --> 00:01:12,970
[SPEAKER_01]: As a matter of fact, I cheated a little bit.

29
00:01:12,990 --> 00:01:15,831
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the only time I ever cheated in baseball.

30
00:01:15,891 --> 00:01:22,072
[SPEAKER_01]: I played on Saturdays under the name of Charlie Graham for the St. Louis Screw Company.

31
00:01:22,212 --> 00:01:26,693
[SPEAKER_01]: And on Sundays, I played under my own name, Charlie Grimm,

32
00:01:28,563 --> 00:01:31,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Camelotto Supply Company.

33
00:01:31,286 --> 00:01:32,867
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how I got my start.

34
00:01:33,347 --> 00:01:36,530
[SPEAKER_01]: I was signed by Philadelphia Athletics in 1916.

35
00:01:36,570 --> 00:01:38,612
[SPEAKER_01]: They have a scout there.

36
00:01:38,672 --> 00:01:40,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Meyer Thomas, yeah.

37
00:01:40,394 --> 00:01:41,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, Meyer, the old catcher.

38
00:01:41,435 --> 00:01:43,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the old catcher, Connie Meyer.

39
00:01:43,977 --> 00:01:46,339
[SPEAKER_01]: So I joined them, played about three months there.

40
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:54,887
[SPEAKER_01]: And that in 1917, as you know, the war, they sent me to Durham, but I never played in Durham.

41
00:01:55,459 --> 00:01:59,081
[SPEAKER_01]: I only played one year in the minor leagues, and that was Little Rock, Arkansas.

42
00:01:59,281 --> 00:02:00,982
[SPEAKER_01]: What year was that?

43
00:02:01,002 --> 00:02:02,823
[SPEAKER_01]: That'd be in 1918.

44
00:02:03,524 --> 00:02:06,866
[SPEAKER_01]: In the fall of 1918, I was sold to the Pittsburgh Pirates.

45
00:02:06,966 --> 00:02:08,987
[SPEAKER_02]: What happened with your Connie Mack arrangement?

46
00:02:09,007 --> 00:02:10,068
[SPEAKER_02]: Did they let you go?

47
00:02:10,128 --> 00:02:10,468
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

48
00:02:10,668 --> 00:02:11,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

49
00:02:11,408 --> 00:02:13,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I was a free agent.

50
00:02:14,210 --> 00:02:15,671
[SPEAKER_02]: You were pretty young then, too, buddy.

51
00:02:15,711 --> 00:02:16,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

52
00:02:16,311 --> 00:02:19,013
[SPEAKER_01]: I was about 16 or 17 years old.

53
00:02:19,033 --> 00:02:20,754
[SPEAKER_02]: How'd you like playing with Connie?

54
00:02:20,954 --> 00:02:22,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he was a great man.

55
00:02:22,095 --> 00:02:23,035
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a great man.

56
00:02:23,115 --> 00:02:24,516
[SPEAKER_01]: As a matter of fact, the three months

57
00:02:25,061 --> 00:02:29,424
[SPEAKER_01]: that I spent there was quite a baseball education for me.

58
00:02:30,184 --> 00:02:31,325
[SPEAKER_01]: He was just a wonderful man.

59
00:02:31,345 --> 00:02:33,387
[SPEAKER_01]: He never called anybody by their first name.

60
00:02:34,147 --> 00:02:40,712
[SPEAKER_01]: That included fellas like Jimmy Dykes and Amos Strunk, who were on that 1916 ball club.

61
00:02:40,772 --> 00:02:42,313
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll call them all son.

62
00:02:46,155 --> 00:02:48,577
[SPEAKER_02]: He already was old enough to be their father, I guess.

63
00:02:48,677 --> 00:02:49,317
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh my, sure.

64
00:02:49,337 --> 00:02:51,579
[SPEAKER_02]: There were a lot of those fellas on that club.

65
00:02:52,936 --> 00:02:53,997
[SPEAKER_02]: Who was their first baseman?

66
00:02:54,517 --> 00:02:55,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Stuffy McGinnis.

67
00:02:56,258 --> 00:02:57,638
[SPEAKER_02]: Did he give you any tips?

68
00:02:57,799 --> 00:03:05,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I watched him, really the man that I followed when I was in St. Louis as a kid was Hal Chase.

69
00:03:05,843 --> 00:03:07,343
[SPEAKER_01]: He took quite an interest in me.

70
00:03:07,403 --> 00:03:08,004
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that right?

71
00:03:08,024 --> 00:03:09,304
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he was my idol.

72
00:03:09,785 --> 00:03:11,806
[SPEAKER_02]: He was called the greatest fielding first baseman.

73
00:03:11,866 --> 00:03:13,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, he was, he was great.

74
00:03:13,146 --> 00:03:15,287
[SPEAKER_02]: He had some other flaws though.

75
00:03:15,327 --> 00:03:18,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he got into a lot of trouble.

76
00:03:18,149 --> 00:03:19,229
[SPEAKER_02]: But he could really field.

77
00:03:19,249 --> 00:03:22,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he was a great fielding first baseman, not a bad hitter.

78
00:03:23,435 --> 00:03:38,503
[SPEAKER_01]: But he knew all the fundamentals of the game and he took a lot of interest in me and around old Robinson Field and Sportsman's Park in St. Louis and he became my idol.

79
00:03:38,963 --> 00:03:49,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course as I played ball in the National League all my life why Babe Ruth was my idol because we became real good friends as I got a little older.

80
00:03:52,355 --> 00:03:53,936
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, George's sister was pregnant.

81
00:03:54,016 --> 00:03:54,437
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah.

82
00:03:54,457 --> 00:03:58,740
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a great ball player, but I was established then in the National League.

83
00:03:58,860 --> 00:04:03,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Never saw much of George, only around St. Louis.

84
00:04:04,724 --> 00:04:10,208
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, how did the Cardinals get you then in 1918?

85
00:04:10,488 --> 00:04:14,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I just went there in 1918.

86
00:04:14,932 --> 00:04:16,533
[SPEAKER_01]: That was the war year, of course.

87
00:04:17,554 --> 00:04:19,175
[SPEAKER_01]: I went there and just ranched.

88
00:04:19,195 --> 00:04:21,697
[SPEAKER_01]: Ricky was the general manager.

89
00:04:22,494 --> 00:04:28,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Jack Hendricks was the manager and I just hung around the ballpark there and didn't get into any games at all.

90
00:04:28,836 --> 00:04:32,017
[SPEAKER_01]: And they're the ones that sent me to Little Rock.

91
00:04:32,037 --> 00:04:36,758
[SPEAKER_01]: And I played one year for Little Rock and was sold to the Pirates in the fall of 1918.

92
00:04:36,998 --> 00:04:43,280
[SPEAKER_02]: They had some scouts looking at you or just how did that develop?

93
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:44,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh yeah, that developed.

94
00:04:45,200 --> 00:04:47,921
[SPEAKER_01]: Bobby Wallace, quite a few scouts.

95
00:04:49,660 --> 00:04:50,981
[SPEAKER_02]: What did you think of Branch Rickey?

96
00:04:52,342 --> 00:04:54,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, as time went on, I admired the man.

97
00:04:54,844 --> 00:04:55,764
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought he was Mr.

98
00:04:55,824 --> 00:04:56,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Baseball.

99
00:04:56,945 --> 00:04:58,366
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a great fundamentalist.

100
00:04:59,547 --> 00:05:11,594
[SPEAKER_01]: And of course, if you wanted a little money off of him or something, he'd make you cry with all the bad deals that happened to him through his baseball life.

101
00:05:11,614 --> 00:05:16,077
[SPEAKER_01]: But mentally, to me, he was Mr.

102
00:05:16,117 --> 00:05:16,658
[SPEAKER_01]: Baseball.

103
00:05:18,504 --> 00:05:25,009
[SPEAKER_01]: If anybody followed him right on down the line and followed on his line, he became a good, smart baseball man.

104
00:05:25,789 --> 00:05:38,658
[SPEAKER_01]: Who managed the Pirates when you went up there in 19- Well, they had George Gibson, Hugo Besdag, the old, I think, the old football coach, and Bill McKechnie.

105
00:05:38,678 --> 00:05:40,279
[SPEAKER_01]: They had quite a few managers there.

106
00:05:40,420 --> 00:05:43,422
[SPEAKER_02]: Who did you succeed as first baseman?

107
00:05:43,482 --> 00:05:45,663
[SPEAKER_01]: One of the Grimes boys.

108
00:05:45,743 --> 00:05:47,064
[SPEAKER_02]: Ray Grimes?

109
00:05:47,124 --> 00:05:48,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Ray Grimes, yeah.

110
00:05:50,577 --> 00:05:51,457
[SPEAKER_02]: Who else is on that team?

111
00:05:51,477 --> 00:05:52,598
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you remember that team very well?

112
00:05:52,658 --> 00:06:01,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, they had George with it, Barnhart, Wagner was through with it, Max Carey, Honest Wagner was just going out.

113
00:06:01,804 --> 00:06:06,348
[SPEAKER_01]: I was a good friend of Honest as he took a liking to him too.

114
00:06:06,708 --> 00:06:12,432
[SPEAKER_02]: They say when rookies first come up, the old timers don't give much help or break because they're taking their jobs away.

115
00:06:12,472 --> 00:06:13,032
[SPEAKER_01]: That's right.

116
00:06:13,052 --> 00:06:16,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, a fellow like Wagner, they've been at the end of the rope and

117
00:06:17,307 --> 00:06:20,790
[SPEAKER_01]: And all you had to do was listen to them a little bit, and they'd take a liking to you.

118
00:06:21,210 --> 00:06:27,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, it took me a long time to get in the batting cage, because they wouldn't leave you in there in those days.

119
00:06:27,576 --> 00:06:29,998
[SPEAKER_01]: They wouldn't leave you in there at all.

120
00:06:30,018 --> 00:06:30,818
[SPEAKER_01]: You had to do it.

121
00:06:30,878 --> 00:06:37,664
[SPEAKER_01]: As a matter of fact, I have a catching mitt I caught batting practice in spring training, just to have something to do with the ball club.

122
00:06:39,265 --> 00:06:40,726
[SPEAKER_02]: You went to spring training in 19?

123
00:06:40,887 --> 00:06:42,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, Hot Springs, Arkansas.

124
00:06:44,443 --> 00:06:46,204
[SPEAKER_02]: That must have been a big thrill.

125
00:06:46,304 --> 00:06:48,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that was my first big thrill of baseball.

126
00:06:48,925 --> 00:06:50,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Did they give you much money that first year?

127
00:06:50,626 --> 00:06:50,886
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

128
00:06:51,507 --> 00:06:56,829
[SPEAKER_01]: I just got, I think it was somewhere around $2,500 a year.

129
00:06:58,690 --> 00:07:01,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, how were the Pirates that year?

130
00:07:01,832 --> 00:07:02,372
[SPEAKER_01]: How'd they do?

131
00:07:02,692 --> 00:07:06,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we were right up there once I became a regular first baseman.

132
00:07:07,395 --> 00:07:08,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Always in contention.

133
00:07:09,736 --> 00:07:13,518
[SPEAKER_01]: And football players like Carson Bigby, Max Carey,

134
00:07:14,431 --> 00:07:25,775
[SPEAKER_01]: And George Whitted, Cotton Tierney, Waller Schmidt, and I had a good pitch in staff, Lee Meadows, Babe Adams, all those old timers were great pitchers.

135
00:07:27,155 --> 00:07:29,776
[SPEAKER_02]: When did you start playing basketball?

136
00:07:29,796 --> 00:07:31,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I started that when I was a kid.

137
00:07:32,637 --> 00:07:39,099
[SPEAKER_01]: As a matter of fact, I had a lot of fun all through my baseball career.

138
00:07:39,220 --> 00:07:43,381
[SPEAKER_01]: I was a relaxed ball player, and when I managed, I relaxed my ball player.

139
00:07:44,825 --> 00:07:46,765
[SPEAKER_02]: You had a number of combos throughout your career.

140
00:07:46,785 --> 00:07:48,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes, yes, yes.

141
00:07:48,726 --> 00:07:51,927
[SPEAKER_01]: As a matter of fact, we had as good a band as the old Gas House gang.

142
00:07:52,567 --> 00:07:53,347
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought we did.

143
00:07:53,727 --> 00:07:54,487
[SPEAKER_02]: Who were some of them?

144
00:07:55,127 --> 00:07:57,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, Barney Fryberg was one of them.

145
00:07:57,508 --> 00:07:58,568
[SPEAKER_01]: What did he play?

146
00:07:58,588 --> 00:08:01,169
[SPEAKER_01]: Third base.

147
00:08:01,289 --> 00:08:02,309
[SPEAKER_01]: He played the mandolin.

148
00:08:02,609 --> 00:08:06,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Cliff Heathcote, the old outfielder, he played a guitar.

149
00:08:06,350 --> 00:08:07,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Hack Miller played a guitar.

150
00:08:08,811 --> 00:08:10,251
[SPEAKER_01]: And I played my banjo.

151
00:08:11,331 --> 00:08:14,092
[SPEAKER_01]: And we had a guy in the club who had a pretty good voice.

152
00:08:15,391 --> 00:08:17,373
[SPEAKER_01]: And he was a pitcher, Al Carlson.

153
00:08:17,733 --> 00:08:18,414
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

154
00:08:18,574 --> 00:08:19,235
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

155
00:08:19,255 --> 00:08:23,579
[SPEAKER_02]: What was this Paderewski story?

156
00:08:23,959 --> 00:08:28,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that Paderewski story was something we were trained at Pazza Robles out in California.

157
00:08:29,745 --> 00:08:34,429
[SPEAKER_01]: I used to, after workout, I used to practice my banjo a little bit.

158
00:08:34,449 --> 00:08:38,293
[SPEAKER_01]: And there was a knock at the door one day, and Andy Lux, our trainer, was right there.

159
00:08:39,093 --> 00:08:40,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Knock came in the door, and it was

160
00:08:42,308 --> 00:08:46,429
[SPEAKER_01]: who was probably Paderewski's secretary, knocked on the door.

161
00:08:46,449 --> 00:08:53,290
[SPEAKER_01]: And Andy went to the door, and he says, this gentleman there told me that Mr. Paderewski's upstairs.

162
00:08:53,350 --> 00:08:54,950
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't know who Paderewski was.

163
00:08:54,990 --> 00:08:59,151
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, Mr. Paderewski's right above us here in his room.

164
00:08:59,171 --> 00:09:06,212
[SPEAKER_01]: And he's having his afternoon nap and asked you to quiet down or else quit playing that banjo.

165
00:09:06,352 --> 00:09:07,833
[SPEAKER_01]: And so I began.

166
00:09:09,977 --> 00:09:24,303
[SPEAKER_01]: quit, and about a couple days later after practice, I had a little time lying down, and Padruski was a great pianist, as you know, and I heard this beautiful flow of music coming out of the window on the second floor.

167
00:09:24,923 --> 00:09:36,488
[SPEAKER_01]: I went to living on the first floor, and I called up Andy Lashaw to come down, and I said, would you mind going up and knocking on Mr. Padruski's door and telling Mr. Charlie Grimm, first baseman for the Pittsburgh Pirates,

168
00:09:36,902 --> 00:09:40,903
[SPEAKER_01]: is taking his afternoon's nap and ask him to quit playing on the piano.

169
00:09:41,603 --> 00:09:42,203
[SPEAKER_02]: Which he did.

170
00:09:44,164 --> 00:09:54,306
[SPEAKER_01]: I finally met the man and he got quite a kick out of it.

171
00:09:54,606 --> 00:09:58,927
[SPEAKER_02]: How'd you like McKechnie?

172
00:09:59,447 --> 00:10:02,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Bill McKechnie was a great guy.

173
00:10:02,268 --> 00:10:06,409
[SPEAKER_01]: A lot of critics thought he was too Pollyannic but

174
00:10:07,614 --> 00:10:13,236
[SPEAKER_01]: I followed his way of managing quite a bit, but I liked to play for Bill.

175
00:10:13,316 --> 00:10:14,956
[SPEAKER_01]: I played for Bill for quite a while.

176
00:10:16,056 --> 00:10:20,458
[SPEAKER_01]: But the best manager I played for was Joe McCarthy on the coach in 1929.

177
00:10:20,558 --> 00:10:22,038
[SPEAKER_02]: Why was that?

178
00:10:22,878 --> 00:10:27,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he was more of a... he used psychology.

179
00:10:28,720 --> 00:10:34,662
[SPEAKER_01]: And by this time I became a character like Gabby Harden and Hack Wilson, and he treated everybody

180
00:10:35,212 --> 00:10:39,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Psychologically, he treated each individual the way he should be treated.

181
00:10:40,194 --> 00:10:48,537
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the way I phrased myself around managing my ballplayers when I became manager of the Chicago Cubs.

182
00:10:49,177 --> 00:10:56,639
[SPEAKER_02]: Tell me, during those 20s while you were with Fitzgerald and then over with the Cubs, did you think you might want to be a manager someday?

183
00:10:56,999 --> 00:10:58,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Never gave it a thought.

184
00:10:59,240 --> 00:11:02,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Joan McCarthy made the captain out of me.

185
00:11:02,601 --> 00:11:04,522
[SPEAKER_01]: I took those chores over for him.

186
00:11:05,437 --> 00:11:09,639
[SPEAKER_01]: And it took about, oh, I guess a year and a half, two years.

187
00:11:11,540 --> 00:11:12,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Pardon me.

188
00:11:14,061 --> 00:11:14,842
[SPEAKER_01]: I caught up with it.

189
00:11:14,982 --> 00:11:19,444
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought I was getting pretty smart myself, but I wasn't looking for anybody's job.

190
00:11:20,484 --> 00:11:26,247
[SPEAKER_01]: And Roger Hornsby took over McCarthy's job after the 1929 World Series.

191
00:11:27,168 --> 00:11:31,750
[SPEAKER_01]: I had no visions at all of managing the Cubs until

192
00:11:32,664 --> 00:11:37,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Ornsby got in trouble with Bill Beck, Sr., who was president of the Chicago Cubs at that time.

193
00:11:38,549 --> 00:11:40,651
[SPEAKER_01]: And we lost a tough ball game in Brooklyn.

194
00:11:40,711 --> 00:11:45,015
[SPEAKER_01]: And we went from Brooklyn over to Philadelphia to open up a series.

195
00:11:46,757 --> 00:11:49,679
[SPEAKER_01]: And Bob Lewis, our traveling secretary, came down to my room.

196
00:11:49,719 --> 00:11:52,442
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, come on up to my room.

197
00:11:52,842 --> 00:11:55,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Bill Beck wants to have a talk chat with you.

198
00:11:57,247 --> 00:12:03,310
[SPEAKER_01]: And I went up to the room, waited for his call in Bob Lewis's room, and he asked me if I wanted to manage the club.

199
00:12:04,490 --> 00:12:05,951
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, well, what happened to Hornsby?

200
00:12:05,971 --> 00:12:07,711
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, we're letting Hornsby go.

201
00:12:08,192 --> 00:12:10,933
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, then I'll take the job.

202
00:12:10,953 --> 00:12:12,953
[SPEAKER_02]: Just out of the blue?

203
00:12:13,133 --> 00:12:14,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, just out of the blue.

204
00:12:16,595 --> 00:12:18,296
[SPEAKER_01]: And we went from there.

205
00:12:18,356 --> 00:12:19,336
[SPEAKER_01]: We finished.

206
00:12:20,476 --> 00:12:20,877
[SPEAKER_01]: That was 1944.

207
00:12:25,219 --> 00:12:35,326
[SPEAKER_01]: And in 1945, not 45, in 1932, we won the pennant in 1932 and played the Yankees and never won a ball game off of them.

208
00:12:35,706 --> 00:12:38,948
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, when you took over, the team was always in second place, right?

209
00:12:38,968 --> 00:12:40,669
[SPEAKER_02]: So they weren't really that far out of it.

210
00:12:41,170 --> 00:12:44,232
[SPEAKER_01]: Greatest break a guy ever had in baseball.

211
00:12:44,332 --> 00:12:44,992
[SPEAKER_01]: I had there.

212
00:12:45,152 --> 00:12:48,555
[SPEAKER_01]: I looked at that ball club and everybody was ready.

213
00:12:48,595 --> 00:12:52,077
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, I was the player manager and I stayed that way for some time.

214
00:12:52,510 --> 00:12:57,932
[SPEAKER_02]: Was it a little awkward being a player and then being elevated above your fellows like that?

